title ALES83 — Lotus's sample-rate decimator, the bit-crush's twin on the time axis
prompt recur baton — take the next agent-doable Lindy-first crux-first lap on the road, send each finished increment, stop at custody gates
think REMEMBER named DREY as the living edge, yet git HEAD is ALES82 (lotus tube drive) — the loop climbs the Lotus DSP suite one signed module per round, faster than the card refreshes; the true next crux is git-current, not card-current.
think ALES81 (bit-crush) and ALES82 (tube) both name the sample-rate decimator as the DRIVE family's remaining rung — the bit-crush's twin on the OTHER axis (crush lowers resolution on the value axis; decimate lowers rate on the time axis). Self-approved, no custody gate.
think The decimator is a zero-order hold: partition the span into runs of `hold` anchored at start, pin every sample in a run to its first (the anchor). hold=1 is the identity; the last run may be partial. It writes only existing samples (a copy), so the output is a legal i16 by construction and no saturate is owed.
think Honestly named the family's first map that is not memoryless PER SAMPLE (an output equals its run's anchor, a neighbor) yet memoryless PER RUN — a run-aligned block decimation equals the whole — and idempotent. In-place safe left to right: anchor <= i and the anchor is written to itself before any later index in its run reads it.
obs Followed the sibling pattern exactly (crush.rye, tube.rye, ales_tube_witness.rish): module + design doc + witness + README status-line sync + session log, one signed commit. Six laws + fault law proven in the selftest; witness GREEN; tame_style_check clean.
loom witness=ales_decimate_witness selftest=GREEN laws=7 module_lines=207
